**No, Fresh Claude Trial 2 does not demonstrate measurable overhead in the reviewed document.** The text asserts a ~0.5 overhead figure tied to “6,500-word bootstrap vs 2,000 needed, 5 rounds to 98% convergence, VuDu logs,” yet supplies none of the underlying measurements. No raw token counts, timestamped logs, baseline task definitions, or calculation method appear. The overhead values (0.3/0.4/0.5) are presented as pre-computed constants rather than derived results.

Evidence quality is therefore at the level of self-reported assertion. It lacks:
- verifiable data artifacts (excerpted logs, CSV exports, or reproducible scripts);
- explicit operational definitions (what counts as a “round,” how convergence is scored, what the minimal 2,000-word comparator task was);
- uncertainty quantification or sensitivity checks.

The document defers all substantiation to an external file (AUDITOR_OVERHEAD_METRICS.md) that is not provided here. Until that file is examined and its methodology shown to be transparent, falsifiable, and replicable, the overhead claims remain unverified assertions rather than empirical measurements. Under an empirical lens this falls short of even moderate evidence standards; it is closer to design documentation than to data.
